    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Carved out of Alexander's conquests by his general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I</a>, the Seleucid Empire stretched from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> at its height.</li><li>Its many new Greek cities spread Hellenistic language and culture across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> for centuries.</li><li>Defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, Parthian conquest of the east and endless dynastic civil war reduced it to a Syrian rump that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> annexed in 64-63 BCE.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I</a> traded eastern lands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya">Chandragupta Maurya</a> for 500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a>, which helped him win the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ipsus">Battle of Ipsus</a> in 301 BCE.</p></aside>
    </section>

